#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "c34ce110-10f4-5bee-97ac-bc4209c6938e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5094" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/025bf6fd6149dfc55da76e1dbb72e3eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0)$, and passes through the point $(6,-4)$.\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-4)$, so we can substitute $x = 6$ and $y = -4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\n6^{2} &= 4p(-4) \\\\\n36 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\\nx^{2} &= -9y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "291b9880-227d-5d7a-b3b0-5f933abeb8fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5095" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,-4)$." :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,-4)$, so we can substitute $x = 2$ and $y = -4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\n2^{2} &= 4p(-4) \\\\\n4 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\\nx^{2} &= -y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5ef63d62-a163-52ea-b92f-c1e0131e9c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5096" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/d522d0281a2935fc62efdddf7cdf5df0.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,4)$, and passes through the point $(4,2)$.\n\nWe know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,4)\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4p(y-4)\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p$. We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,2)$, so we can substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y - 4) \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(2 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -8p \\\\\np &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y - 4) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-2)(y - 4) \\\\\nx^{2} &= -8(y - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "c62e52ff-4940-5529-9664-78636bfaaa7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5097" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(4,6)$." :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,-2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 0)^{2} &= 4p(y - (-2)) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p$. We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,6)$, so we can substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2) \\\\\n(4)^{2} &= 4p(6 + 2) \\\\\n16 &= 32p \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(\\frac{1}{2})(y + 2) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 2(y + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8bf18c-3fd6-5eff-a573-62b18802f685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093939} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf945ca-a6f2-53cc-b431-4ae502771cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093940} {:answer/id #uuid "014288f8-1dc5-57f5-bf2e-340c82ed75ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093941} {:answer/id #uu